    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{2}\\sec t,\\,z\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n3y^2-x^2=6,\\qquad y\\ge \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Divide the Cartesian equation by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{2}-\\frac{x^2}{6}=1\n$$\n\nThe base curve uses $y$ and $x$; $z$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{6}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\sec t)^2}{2}-1=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nChoose $x=\\sqrt{6}\\tan t$, which takes every real value on the given interval. This is the positive-tangent orientation used in the choices. The scale is $\\sqrt{6}=\\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{6}\\tan t,\\qquad \\mathbf{f}(t,z)=\\langle \\sqrt{6}\\tan t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\sec t,\\,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\sec t)^2}{2}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{6}\\tan t)^2}{6}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $z$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the first component is $\\sqrt{6}\\tan t$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "b0be70f3-1492-5802-8a86-f4997cdc5107",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
